<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Qingzhao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1084–c. 1155</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Yi'an Jushi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Qingzhao was a Chinese poet and essayist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, considered one of the greatest poets in Chinese history. Born in 1084 in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> to a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>, she was known in elite circles by 1101, when she married Zhao Mingcheng, with whom she collected books, inscriptions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jin-song-wars">Jin–Song wars</a> the couple fled south after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkang-incident">fall of Kaifeng</a> in 1127; Zhao died in 1129, and Li settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, completing his Catalogue of Inscriptions on Metal and Stone and writing an afterword on their life. Only about a hundred of her poems survive, mostly in the ci form, her later work marked by loss and nostalgia.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Qingzhao, art name Yi'an Jushi, was born in 1084 in Qi Prefecture, Jingdong East Circuit, in present-day Zhangqiu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. Her father, Li Gefei, was an academic professor and well-known essayist who belonged to a poetry and literary circle led by Su Shi, and her mother, also a poet, descended from the prime minister Wang Kung-ch'en. The family's large collection of books gave her a comprehensive education. As a teenager she began a career as a poet by writing two poems in the shi form to rhyme with a friend's poem. Her first ci poem, &quot;A Dream Song&quot;, recalls her hometown before her marriage and was written at about 16 or 17.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her poetry was already well known in elite circles by 1101, when, aged eighteen, she married Zhao Mingcheng. They shared a love of poetry, literature, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, painting and bronze and stone sculpture, and they collected inscriptions and books and wrote poems about bronze vessels of the Shang and Zhou dynasties. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jin-song-wars">Jin–Song wars</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> fell in 1127, fighting reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and their house was burned. The couple fled south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> with many possessions and lived for a year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. Zhao died of typhoid fever in 1129 on the way to an official post, and Li never recovered.</p><p>She settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> government had made its new capital, and kept working on her husband's Catalogue of Inscriptions on Metal and Stone, describing their married life and flight in an afterword to the work, which was published after his death. According to some contemporary accounts she was briefly married to a man named Zhang Ruzhou, who treated her badly, and she divorced him within months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Only around a hundred of her poems are known to survive, mostly in the ci form, which follows musical patterns and allows personal feeling. A few shi poems survive, as do the afterword and a study of ci poetry. Her work falls into two parts, divided by her move south: early poems on a maiden's feelings, close to love poems, and later ones tied to the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a>, marked by nostalgic memories of her husband and her hometown. She is credited with the first detailed critique of the metrics of Chinese poetry and was regarded as a master of the subtle and concise style. It is widely believed that she died around the age of 71, but no records of this remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Qingzhao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Writing flourished too: the poet <b>Li Qingzhao</b> (1084–1151) was famous in her lifetime, and the geographer Fan Chengda (1126–1193) made travel literature popular.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The lyrics of the Song dynasty, by poets such as Su Shi and <b>Li Qingzhao</b>, and the prose essays of writers such as Han Yu and Ouyang Xiu, who revived an “ancient style” of clear and vigorous prose, are classics still read today.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Educated mothers taught their sons, and <b>Li Qingzhao</b> became one of China’s greatest poets.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zhangqiu District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.76,117.49&amp;year=1130" aria-label="Zhangqiu District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7499 839 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Li Qingzhao"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7949 1109h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7949 1109h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.76,117.49&amp;year=1130">What was here in 1130? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
